WEBVTT

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.160
Let's knit a test collar with a sandwich

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.660
band that can be used
to cover a cut and sew edge.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.820
This could also be used to attach
a collar to a shaped neckline.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.020
Now, this is not a beginner project.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.640
There are a number of steps to construct
this collar, and there are also a number

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:28.800
of skills you must master
or review before attempting it.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.940
The first one is a neat
full needle, rib cast on.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.060
The cast on edge of your collar will be
the focus of your garment,

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.640
so be sure you practice and knit
a cast on that you like.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.720
I used the broken toe cast on.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.780
It starts with three slip rows.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.940
Number two, be familiar
with knitting and full needle rib.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.780
Remember, because all needles are on work,

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:07.100
you'll need a thinner yarn than you might
normally knit with on your machine.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.200
Number three,

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:12.700
you must be familiar with setting
your machine for tubular knitting.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:18.050
We will knit in the round for the pocket
or that sandwich section of this project.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.130
Plus, we will scrap off the ribber

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.420
stitches, leaving the main
bed stitches in work.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.680
You also need to be comfortable

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.100
with manually pulling stitches
through the knitting.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.360
Now, this can get tricky,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:36.240
so remove any distractions,
get a good night's sleep, and focus.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.840
You also need to be comfortable

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:44.180
with binding off in the stitch-by-stitch
method with long loops.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:49.840
Of course, you could scrap off and
hand-knit to secure the last stitches.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.820
Now, if you're feeling comfortable with
all of these skills, let's get started.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.490
Instead of creating a cut and sew
neckline for our test.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.930
I'm just using a surged edge of a swatch.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.900
It isn't even the same yarn as my collar.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:11.640
No matter, we just want
to practice the steps.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.140
First, determine how many needles
you'll need for your collar.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:21.780
Hold the edge up against the machine,
stretching it slightly.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.500
Set your machine for full needle rib. Half

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.660
pitch, and put the required number
of needles in work on both beds.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.680
Cast on with the method of your choice.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.890
I'm working a broken toe cast on,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.820
which will give me a strand to tighten so
that I can steam and set that cast on edge.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.380
Now, if you aren't familiar with this
cast on, I suggest you give it a try.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:52.780
Next, measure a collar on a sweater or
a shirt to determine the length you like.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.060
Remember, it's going to fold over,

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.740
so you might want to make
it a little bit longer.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.780
This is a good time to experiment.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:06.600
Knit the desired length of your collar.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.180
Set your machine for circular knitting.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.880
For example, slip to the left on the main

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:24.420
bed, slip to the right on the ribber,
and knit about a half inch worth of rows.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.080
Remember, it takes two passes

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.780
of the carriage to complete
a row in tubular knitting.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:35.520
I knit a total of six rows,
12 passes of the carriage.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.020
We'll scrap off the ribber stitches only

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:50.680
by canceling slip on the ribber and setting
your main bed to slip in both directions.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:57.700
Remove the main yarn from the yarn feeder
and thread the carriage with scrap yarn.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:02.100
Knit 4-6 rows of scrap on the ribber only.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.660
Notice that the stitches are going
to bunch up a little bit,

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:11.980
so don't knit too many rows and make
your scrap a good contrasting color.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.860
When you're done there,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:20.180
after knitting just a few rows,
drop those stitches and drop your ribber.

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:24.620
Notice the pocket that's been created.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.220
Next, we'll attach the garment edge.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:34.260
With the right side facing up, position
the garment edge across the main bed.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.300
The cut edge of your
neckline will be facing you.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.320
Press that cut edge against the sinker

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:46.320
post to position the cut edge
inside that pocket or sandwich.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.760
Push the main bed needles through the neck

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.560
edge, and

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.000
re-hang the scrapped-off pocket stitches

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:09.760
from the ribber and position them
in the hooks of the needles.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:20.480
Make sure you close all of the laches and
carefully pull the stitches through both

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:25.600
the neck edge and the stitches
that are remaining on that main bed.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.900
I removed the waist yarn to confirm
that I picked up all of my stitches.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:42.240
Oops, I missed one.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.240
You must take care with the laches so they
don't catch on the knitting

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.340
and the stitches remain
in the hooks of the needles.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.020
This can be a little tedious.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.840
Take your time.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:06.840
Flip the garment towards you and carefully
pop the knitting off the gate pegs.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.440
Handknit a loose row
with fairly long loops.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:27.600
I just hand-knit some large loops and then
pulled loop through loop to bind off.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.000
Now, if you prefer not to have such

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.640
a visible bind off, you could
sew those stitches in place.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.000
With all the steps involved,

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:47.380
you can see how things can get a bit
confusing and easily go wrong.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.760
Before trying this technique on a garment,

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:55.420
I suggest you knit at least one other test
collar using the edge of a swatch,

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:59.200
or maybe get brave and use
a sample cut and sew neckline.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:04.880
With a good mental picture,
I'm sure you will have success.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.140
First, we knit the collar,
leaving it on the machine.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:13.640
Then we knit in the round
to create the sandwich pocket.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.660
We then positioned the cut edge

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:21.220
in that sandwich and pushed
the needles through the cut edge.

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.180
We pulled one-half of the sandwich

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:29.180
stitches through that cut edge
and neatly bound off.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:34.040
Have fun knitting your sandwich collar.

